the january trading frenzy of certain  stocks has come under intense scrutiny  as  legislators regulators and others try to  understand what drove the rapid rise and  fall  of these securities gamestop shares in  particular  surge from about 20 to 483  over two weeks in january the price has  since fallen to around 45  the securities and exchange commission  is reviewing the volatility  the department of justice has launched a  probe and the commodity futures trading  commission has opened a preliminary  investigation into certain circumstances  of the rally  also the house financial services  committee is reviewing if the actions of  brokerages and online communities  require more oversight  central to these lines of inquiry are  questions of market manipulation  and the actions brokerages took in  response to the rally  but understanding whether these actions  cross the line takes time  and isn't always easy to determine  market manipulation in a nutshell is a  scheme  to create some kind of an artificial  price  so you can you can do that different  ways  there are different tactics that traders  can use to try to  artificially pump up the price of the  stock and then benefit  by selling into this sort of  frenzy that they've created dave  michaels covers white-collar crime and  financial enforcement for the wall  street journal  and says market manipulation schemes  have been going on as long as there's  been a market  some are quite time tested meaning  meaning they've been going on for a long  time  and regulators know the recipe and they  know sort of the telltale signs  a well-known example of market  manipulation is the stratton oakmont  pump and dump scheme of the early 90s  dramatized in the movie the wolf of wall  street it involved convincing investors  to bet large sums of money on a stock  inflating the price before stratton  brokers sold shares held by the firm and  its partners  what this trade will do is serve as a  benchmark for  future business kids you feel  comfortable with me now scott carey  curtis axel is a former assistant u.s  attorney  and former staff attorney at the sec  your typical  market manipulation case involves false  statements  by people who somehow want to manipulate  the stock price for their own gain  that's your typical kind of pump and  dump scheme  these false statements can come in the  form of brokers pushing inflated  financial outlooks  or bad actors publishing press releases  with outlandish claims of business  breakthroughs  some commentators have said individuals  coordinating on reddit  largely in the wall street bets forum  openly engaged in a pump and dump scheme  to inflate the price of gamestop  and other securities these allegations  have not come from regulators or law  enforcement  the cftc is looking into whether  misconduct occurred as reddit traders  targeted silver futures and the largest  exchange-traded fund tied to silver  the i-share silver trust but in an  anonymous public forum like reddit  determining the identity of users and  whether their posts were intended to  deceive  others may not be so clear-cut the sec  is a law enforcement agency  and it can seek records from reddit  that would reveal the people behind  these usernames  they would do that by sending a subpoena  for records  to reddit and reddit could agree  to comply with the subpoena and just  hand the records over on the other hand  there are some limitations that the sec  places frankly on itself in terms of  subpoenaing  news agencies or news media and one  question  may be is reddit sort of operating  um in a sense as a news site  and could it put up a first amendment  argument against the subpoena  actions taken by brokerages have also  caught the attention of regulators  the sec's acting chair allison lee told  npr that the agency is also looking into  whether decisions to curb trading at the  peak of the frenzy complied with  regulations  and specifically it's looking at  the reasons that the brokers gave so you  know  it wants to kick the tires on these  explanations and make sure that they're  true  when volatility goes up uh  clearing houses which are an  organization that sort of  uh they sort of collectively share the  risk  of trades defaulting or not happening in  the stock market  those clearinghouses said to the brokers  you need to give us more cash to cover  the risk  some of these brokers couldn't  immediately  provide all the cash that the  clearinghouse wanted and so  the only way to limit that call for cash  or margin  call was to limit new purchases  uh of gamestop federal prosecutors have  subpoenaed information from brokers such  as robin hood  and the brokerage is facing more than 30  civil lawsuits in relation to the  trading restrictions  i am sure that the sec and finra will be  going through their rule books to  determine if  if there were violations of the rules of  course  broker dealers such as robin hood are  claiming that the very reason they had  to halt trading was to comply with the  sec rules  so they may have a very good defense  that what they did was within their  customer agreements that allows them to  stop trading for any reason  experts say the outcomes of these  investigations are not likely to come  soon but the influence of social media  communities adds a new dimension to  trading that will likely receive  more scrutiny there's going to be this  initial flurry of activity  congress will hold hearings you know  they will want to assuage the public  by taking some initial testimony and  looking like they're doing something  but the hard work of making any change  in this area will likely take some time  at least through the games the lens of  gamestop it seems  like social media is becoming a bigger  influencer  of investment decisions that does change  how  how markets behave at least occasionally  at least with respect to  certain stocks it's it's not something  that they can completely ignore
 

